// A migrated std attribute (`std.test.Skip`) is imported and applied like any attribute (D2b — no
// global attribute namespace): `use std.test.{Skip}` then `#[Skip]`. The loader rewrites the
// application to its qualified identity, so `attributes_of::<Skip>()` — whose turbofish resolves to
// the same FQN — materializes it from the reflection manifest. This exercises the whole
// consumer-side path (use projection → loader FQN rewrite → qualified manifest) end to end.
// expect: stdout "flaky"
// expect: exit 0
use std.test.{Skip}

#[Skip("flaky")]
struct Thing { id: int }

attrs = attributes_of::<Skip>();
for a in attrs {
    echo a.value.reason;
}
